```java
class LockFreeCounter {
    private final AtomicInteger value = new AtomicInteger(0);

    void increment() {
        int current, next;
        do {
            current = value.get();
            next = current + 1;
        } while (!value.compareAndSet(current, next));
    }

    int get() { return value.get(); }
}
```

This is Java's `AtomicInteger.compareAndSet`, but the loop shape is universal to CAS-based design: read, compute, attempt swap, and if the swap reports failure, loop back and try again with fresh data -- no lock is acquired or released anywhere in this method.

Under low contention this loop typically succeeds on the first try; under heavy contention it may retry several times, but it never blocks -- every retry is still making forward progress toward eventually succeeding, unlike a thread queued on a lock.
